## Frequently Asked Questions

### How much does a five-page business website cost?

A professionally designed five-page website might fit within a broad budget of several hundred to several thousand dollars, depending on design complexity, content, integrations, and the development approach.

### Can I build a professional website for $200?

For certain limited-scope projects, a template-based landing page or basic starter website may be possible at that budget. A fully custom multi-page website with extensive functionality is unlikely to fit comfortably within the same scope.

### Is Webflow expensive for small businesses?

Webflow costs depend on the required plan, development work, and additional services. For some businesses, its managed platform approach may simplify website operations.

### Is WordPress completely free?

The WordPress software is free and open source, but hosting, domains, premium tools, development, and maintenance may involve costs.

### Do I have to pay every month for my website?

Not necessarily every month, but most websites have recurring expenses such as hosting, platform subscriptions, domain renewals, or maintenance. Billing schedules vary.

### How long does it take to build a professional website?

Timelines vary based on the number of pages, design requirements, content readiness, integrations, approvals, and technical complexity.

### Can I start with a small website and expand later?

Yes. A well-planned starter website can grow alongside your business, provided its platform and structure can support your future requirements.

## Final Thoughts

The cost of a professional website in 2026 depends on much more than the number of pages.

Design quality, content, functionality, integrations, technology, and ongoing maintenance all influence the overall investment.

For some businesses, a simple landing page is enough to get started.

Others need a more comprehensive website that supports marketing, customer enquiries, content management, or e-commerce.

The goal shouldn't be to purchase the most expensive website or automatically choose the cheapest option.

**The goal is to invest in a website that solves the right problems for your business.**

Start by defining what your website needs to accomplish, then choose an approach that fits your goals and budget.

